dw_mmc: a live instrument for the SD per-request floor, and where the time
actually goes

WHY THIS EXISTS

The ~2.0-2.5 ms per-request SD floor has been "explained" several times and
never localised, because the instruments meant to localise it were dead:

  - req_timing, cmd_probe and busy_wait have DEFINE_SHOW_ATTRIBUTE and are
    never passed to debugfs_create_file(), so none of the three files has
    ever existed;
  - DIAG=0 compiles DEBUG_FS out of the shipping kernel anyway, so even a
    registered file would be absent;
  - /proc/profile drops samples past _etext and the whole request path
    (dw_mmc, blk-mq, mmc core, sched, entry) is in .text..fast, so the
    "dw_mmc is 0.0%" line that was treated as independent confirmation
    could not have seen the driver at any cost.

A sub-agent reported that the accumulators were also never written. That is
only PARTLY true and worth recording: busy_ns, gap, cmd_alone and
cmd_with_data all have writers; ns_issue_cmd, ns_cmd_data and req_count did
not. The stamps t_issue/t_cmd/t_data were being taken and thrown away.

WHAT CHANGED

  - the missing writers for ns_issue_cmd / ns_cmd_data / req_count, at the
    point in dw_mci_interrupt() where the stamps were already being taken;
  - ns_prep / n_prep around dw_mci_submit_data() at its call site, which
    runs BEFORE t_issue is stamped and was therefore outside every previous
    timing window;
  - ns_reset / n_reset inside dw_mci_ctrl_reset();
  - n_cmd_all, counting EVERY command rather than only data commands;
  - readout through module_param_cb(sdprobe) rather than debugfs, because
    DIAG=1 puts the kernel 16 KB past the linux partition AND debugfs costs
    1.14 MB of RAM, which would perturb the page-cache pressure being
    studied. lost_irq_poll already proves the mechanism here.
  - the instrument measures ITSELF at probe: ktime_get_ns() is an MMIO
    write, a polled read and two more reads on this board, and reports 412
    ns per pair, so every figure below can be read net of its own cost.

Cost when not being read: ~3 extra ktime pairs per request, about 1.2 us on
a 2000 us request. Left in deliberately - a dead instrument is how this
question stayed open for so long.

WHAT IT MEASURED (kernel #193, idle board, sdlat O_DIRECT random reads)

Counters differenced around one clean workload, so the averages describe
that workload and not whatever else the system did:

  4 KiB:   issue2cmd   85 us     cmd2data   287 us     -> 372 us in driver
  64 KiB:  issue2cmd  125 us     cmd2data  1311 us
  512 KiB: issue2cmd   78 us     cmd2data  4698 us  (2 requests per read)

  prep  36-45 us      reset 1.2-1.4 us      busy 1571 calls, 0 waits

Userspace sees min 1.911/1.922/1.930/1.976/1.986 ms for 4 KiB across five
runs. So of ~1.95 ms, only 372 us lies between the CMD register write and
the DATA_OVER interrupt. About 1.6 ms is OUTSIDE the driver entirely.

THE THREE HYPOTHESES THIS KILLS

  - the unconditional DMA reset per request: 1.2 us, not hundreds;
  - request preparation (dma_map_sg, descriptors): 36-45 us;
  - dw_mci_wait_while_busy: called 1571 times, waited zero times.

AND ONE IT FALSIFIES BY PREMISE. CMD23 was dismissed on the arithmetic that
a 4 KiB read uses CMD17 with no stop. n_cmd_all says otherwise: 504 commands
for 238 data commands, a ratio of 2.1, so 4 KiB reads are CMD18 + CMD12.
The dismissal may still be right, but not for that reason.

WHERE THE TIME ACTUALLY GOES

rootfs/ctxbench on this kernel: 612 us per pipe round trip, ~303 us per
context switch. rootfs/ctxbench2: thread round trip 595 us against process
606 us - only 2%, so switch_mm and the TLB are NOT the cost.

A blocking read() is at least two switches (block, wake) and, with the mmc
core's hand-off, plausibly four. Four times 303 us is 1.21 ms. Add syscall
entry/exit on a board where generic entry measured 2.83x, and the ~1.6 ms
outside the driver is accounted for without anything SD-specific.

This is the same root cause as two other open items: socket syscalls at
1.3-5.8 ms (af_unix AND inet, "structural"), and the ~8 ms kworker hand-off.
They are one problem - a context switch here costs ~303 us against 2-5 us on
comparable hardware - and it is worth far more than dw_mmc, because it is
paid by every blocking path on the board, including the major faults that
make Doom hitch.

STILL UNRESOLVED, AND IT IS A REAL CONTRADICTION

sdprobe reports bus_hz 40000000, ios_clock 40000000, timing 2 (SD_HS),
width 2 (4-bit). That is a hard 20 MB/s ceiling. The size sweep measures a
marginal transfer rate of ~49 MB/s (512 KiB min 12.254 ms vs 256 KiB 7.011
ms). Both cannot be true. 49 MB/s needs ~98 MHz SDR or ~49 MHz DDR at four
bits, so the CIU divider assumption in clk-esp32s31.c (LS_DIV, an inferred
N+1 encoding) is the place to look. If the clock is not what the driver
believes, every timeout derived from bus_hz is wrong too.
